{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Extract plan_review_decide and the operator-attested cluster into decide_ops.py\n\n## Context / Problem\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py` is at 794 LOC against the 800 cap — SIX lines of headroom\n— and holds two of the five `orchestrator.finalize_verdict` call sites (`:223`, `:760`). It has no\nheadroom guard (sibling ticket adds one).\n\n**The shape was mis-diagnosed on first inspection and the correction changes the fix.** The obvious\nhypothesis is that this is a registry file accreting one op per feature, wanting a domain split along\nop boundaries. THAT IS REFUTED by history: all eight registered ops existed within TWO DAYS of the\nfile's creation (`8b888716ab` 2026-06-25 created five; `c9b959080e` and `ac3b799bf8` on 06-26 added\nthree), and NO new op has been registered here in 35 days across ~28 commits. Growth is 283 -> 794 =\n+511 lines, ALL of it inside existing bodies.\n\nThe pressure is one dominant VERB plus diffuse body growth:\n  plan_review_decide         64 -> 213 LOC   +149  (29% of all growth; 27% of the file)\n  plan_review_coach_inputs        -> 74      +52\n  plan_review_coach          47 -> 88        +41\n  plan_review_assemble_criteria 30 -> 73     +43\n  plan_review_precheck       66 -> 104       +38\n  plan_review_grounding      31 -> 31          0\n  plan_review_passthrough    19 -> 19          0\n`plan_review_decide` is 2.05x the next-largest op, and its comment density is 8% (17/213) — dense\nexecutable code, so there is no prose to compress. +149 of its growth arrived in essentially ONE commit\n(`f373cf4a6e`, \"evaluate direct prerequisites independently\", +129 inside `decide`), and 61 of its 213\nlines now reference `prerequisite`/`focused`/`normalized_coverage`/`invalid_prerequisites` — one named\nconcern threaded through a function whose job is Pass-3 decision.\n\nNote that same commit DID carve out the half with a clean seam (`prerequisite_workflow_ops.py`, 125\nLOC, docstring \"a call-graph seam extracted from workflow_ops\") and left the messy half inline. This\nticket is that split's unfinished other half.\n\n## Approach\nMove `plan_review_decide` plus the operator-attested enrichment cluster and `_ticket_id` into a new\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/decide_ops.py`.\n\n**Why those travel together — a real contract, not size balancing:**\n 1. SOLE-CONSUMER CLOSURE. `enrich_operator_attested` has exactly one call site in `src/`:\n    `workflow_ops.py:542`, inside `decide`. `_norm` and `operator_attested_ac_texts` are called only by\n    `enrich_operator_attested`. Moving either alone creates a cross-module call for nothing.\n 2. LIFECYCLE PHASE. `decide` is the Pass-3 decision-kernel adapter and the ONLY op in the file that\n    touches `review_kernel` at all (`reshape_verifications` `:527`/`:572`, `decide_outcome_counts`\n    `:678`). The enrichment is documented as running \"BEFORE Pass-3 reads it\" (`:538`) — a Pass-3\n    pre-step by construction.\n 3. `_ticket_id` goes to the new side: `decide` is its heaviest caller and `workflow_ops` will import\n    `decide_ops` at module scope for registration anyway. Do NOT duplicate it — a second copy already\n    exists at `workflow/steps.py:36`, which is exactly why a third is wrong.\n\nExpected: `workflow_ops.py` 794 -> ~509 (191 under the 700 target); `decide_ops.py` ~320 (3x the\n100-LOC floor).\n\n**HONEST LIMIT: this relocates the absorber, it does not dissolve it.** `decide` stays 213 lines, just\nin a file with room. The ADR-0056-correct follow-up is a VERB cut inside it — lift the\nprerequisite-coverage normalisation (`:572-629`, ~58 lines, optionally the reclassification at\n`:646-677`) into `prerequisites.py` (280 LOC, 520 free, and already the owner of\n`prerequisite_coverage_model` and `emit_indeterminate`) as a pure function. Deliberately NOT bundled:\nthat one is behaviour-adjacent and needs its own RED-first test on the normalisation contract, whereas\nthis ticket is a pure relocation.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py` — remove the moved units; add the registration import\n  and the re-export block.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/decide_ops.py` — NEW.\n- `tests/unit/` — tighten the workflow_ops headroom assertion to the `< 700` policy number.\n\n## Testing\n**ZERO edits to existing tests. A required edit means the cut is wrong — that is the revert signal.**\nThis is SAFER than the runner.py precedent and the reason is worth stating: there are ZERO\n`monkeypatch.setattr` targets of any form into this module. (Beware `test_code_review_region_gate.py:88`,\nwhich patches the same-named `code_review/workflow_ops.py` — a real trap for a careless grep.) Nine test\nmodules reference the moved names via plain imports or attribute access, all of which resolve through a\nre-export.\n\nNON-NEGOTIABLES carried into the new module, each pinned by an existing test:\n * Reach `orchestrator` by MODULE FORM plus attribute access (`from . import orchestrator`, then\n   `orchestrator.pass3_over_findings`). `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_review_execution_floor_lifecycle.py:85`\n   patches `orchestrator.pass3_over_findings` and then calls `workflow_ops.plan_review_decide` — flattening\n   to a bare-name import silently defeats that patch. This is the pin `test_gate_dispatch_seam.py:106-126`\n   enforces for a sibling module and nothing currently enforces here.\n * Re-export `_OPERATOR_ATTESTED_AC_RE` such that OBJECT IDENTITY survives:\n   `tests/unit/test_det_floor_operator_attested.py:153` asserts `is` against the det-floor regex.\n * `decide_ops` must be a LEAF at import time: no module-scope import of `workflow_ops` (mirrors\n   `prerequisite_workflow_ops.py:20-30`).\n * Add the registration import to `workflow_ops.py` beside `:45`, NOT to `workflow/steps.py` — keeping\n   `steps.py` unaware of plan-review's internal layout, per the existing precedent.\n\nOp NAMES are location-independent: resolution is `registry.get(ctx.step.get(\"uses\"))` against a flat\ndict (`executor.py:673-676`), so `gates/plan-review.yaml` needs NO edit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `plan_review/workflow_ops.py` is at or below 560 LOC; `decide_ops.py` is at least 100 and under 800\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_plan_review_workflow_ops_seam.py` asserts `workflow_ops.py` at the `< 700`\n      policy target. STATED AS AN END STATE, because an earlier version of this criterion described\n      the file's then-current shape (\"currently loops against ONE shared `_HARD_CAP`\") and that\n      description went stale the moment sibling b300 landed — a plan that narrates transient state\n      is falsified by its own siblings. The per-file `_LOC_BOUNDS` table ALREADY EXISTS (b300 built\n      it, holding `config.py` at 700 and `workflow_ops.py` at the staged 800), so the remaining work\n      here is exactly one constant: `(_WORKFLOW_OPS, _HARD_CAP)` becomes\n      `(_WORKFLOW_OPS, _HEADROOM_TARGET)`. Do NOT re-split anything\n\n- [ ] the full suite passes with ZERO edits to any existing test file APART FROM the headroom\n      tightening required above — that one edit is mandated by this plan, so the zero-edit rule is\n      about the RELOCATION: if moving the code requires touching a test's imports, monkeypatch\n      targets or assertions, the cut is wrong and that is the revert signal\n- [ ] a test asserts `decide_ops` has no module-scope import of `workflow_ops` (leaf at import time)\n- [ ] a test asserts `decide_ops` reaches `orchestrator` by attribute access, not bare-name imports, so\n      the lifecycle test's monkeypatch keeps taking effect\n- [ ] a test asserts the moved names are still reachable as attributes of `workflow_ops`, including\n      `_OPERATOR_ATTESTED_AC_RE` by object identity\n- [ ] IN-REPO: `decide_ops.py`'s module docstring states that this RELOCATES the absorber rather\n      than dissolving it (`plan_review_decide` stays 213 lines, just in a file with room) and names\n      the prerequisite-normalisation verb cut as the follow-up, so the next reader meets the caveat\n      in the code\n- [ ] [operator-attested] the same caveat appears in the Gerrit commit message. Tagged because the\n      completion verifier reads repository files ONLY and cannot read git history, so a criterion\n      about a commit message is unconfirmable from the codebase"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785578050657103001,"uuid":"1ecc79cc-4d60-4597-b4c2-6df99299ac55"}